<div class="header-box">
  <h1>THREAD Commerce & Operations REST API</h1>
  <p style="font-size: 14pt; color: #475569; margin: 0;">Comprehensive Architectural Specification & System Documentation</p>
  <p class="meta-text">Course: CSC 4161 / CSC 4182 (Advanced Programming in Web Technologies) | AIUB | Semester: Fall 2026-2027</p>
  <p class="meta-text">Target Role Focus: <b>Administrator (Role D)</b> & System Integration</p>
</div>

<h2>1. Executive Summary & Problem Statement</h2>
<p>
Modern fashion and apparel retailers operate in high-velocity direct-to-consumer (DTC) environments with complex product matrices (e.g., styles, sizes, colors, and stock-keeping units / SKUs). When catalog updates, inventory reservations, customer carts, and checkout pipelines operate in disconnected silos, race conditions cause severe business failures: product overselling, stale pricing, phantom reservations, and inconsistent order ledgers.
</p>
<p>
<b>THREAD</b> is an enterprise-grade single-brand fashion e-commerce REST API built with NestJS, TypeScript, PostgreSQL, and TypeORM. It models end-to-end retail commerce with atomic multi-table transactions, four-role Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), immutable historical price/item snapshots, audit logging, and automated expiry mechanisms.
</p>

<h2>2. System Architecture & High-Level Design</h2>
<p>
The application employs a <b>Modular Monolith</b> architecture. Rather than introducing premature microservice complexity or building isolated fragmented applications, the entire system is hosted in a single cohesive NestJS runtime with strictly defined domain boundaries and zero circular dependencies.
</p>

<div class="callout">
  <b>Core Architectural Principles:</b>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Controller &rarr; Service &rarr; Repository</b>: Controllers handle HTTP serialization and Swagger docs; Services execute domain rules and transactional orchestration; TypeORM Repositories manage database persistence.</li>
    <li><b>Immutable Snapshots</b>: Order items freeze variant names, SKU codes, sizes, colors, and unit prices at purchase time, ensuring historical purchases remain accurate even if catalog prices change later.</li>
    <li><b>Integer Minor Units (BDT)</b>: Monetary values are strictly stored as integer minor units (e.g., 250,000 BDT minor units = 2,500.00 BDT) to eliminate floating-point rounding errors.</li>
    <li><b>Strict Schema Migrations</b>: Automatic schema synchronization is disabled (<code>synchronize: false</code>). The relational schema is versioned across 11 migration files in <code>src/database/migrations</code>.</li>
    <li><b>UUID Primary Keys</b>: All entities use cryptographic UUIDs (<code>gen_random_uuid()</code>) to prevent sequential ID guessing and enumeration attacks.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>3. User Roles & Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)</h2>
<p>
THREAD enforces four authenticated roles plus an unauthenticated Guest browsing mode:
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Role / Actor</th>
      <th>Allowed Capabilities</th>
      <th>Explicit Restrictions & Boundaries</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><b>Guest</b></td>
      <td>Public catalog browsing, product search/filter, customer registration, and login.</td>
      <td>Cannot view customer orders, cart, or access private endpoints.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>CUSTOMER</b></td>
      <td>Manage delivery addresses, cart items, checkout, personal order history, rule-based size advice.</td>
      <td>Cannot alter product prices, modify stock balances, or inspect other users' data.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>STAFF</b></td>
      <td>CRUD on products, variants, categories, collections, and size guides; publish products.</td>
      <td>Cannot mutate stock balances, promote users, or modify payment statuses directly.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>INVENTORY_MANAGER</b></td>
      <td>Receive inventory receipts, execute stock count adjustments, process pack/ship logistics.</td>
      <td>Cannot modify catalog prices, change account roles, or initiate customer refunds.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>ADMIN</b> (Your Role)</td>
      <td>Create and deactivate staff accounts, reassign roles, browse system audit logs, and view platform analytics.</td>
      <td>Must operate through authorized business invariants and cannot bypass financial transaction rules.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>4. Module Breakdown by Team Ownership</h2>
<p>
The repository is partitioned into four clear development domains aligned with the four course project members:
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Developer / Role</th>
