<h2>9. Viva Presentation & Defense Guide</h2>
<div class="callout">
  <b>Top Viva Questions & Strategic Answers:</b>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <b>Q: What did you personally develop in this group project?</b><br>
      <i>A: "I developed the Admin domain (Developer D). My deliverables include the shared authentication foundation with bcrypt password hashing and JWT token issuance, RBAC guards with the custom <code>@Roles()</code> and <code>@Actor()</code> decorators, complete staff account management (<code>/admin/staff</code>), the centralized audit log system with before/after diff tracking (<code>audit_logs</code> table and <code>/admin/audit-logs</code>), and administrative platform analytics."</i>
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Q: How did you implement database transactions in your domain?</b><br>
      <i>A: "In <code>UsersService</code>, staff creation, role modification, and deactivation run inside an atomic database transaction using <code>this.dataSource.transaction()</code>. The user record update and the audit log insertion execute in the same transaction. If audit logging fails, the user changes roll back immediately."</i>
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Q: How do you prevent privilege escalation?</b><br>
      <i>A: "Public registration (<code>POST /auth/register</code>) hardcodes the role to <code>CUSTOMER</code> and ignores any role parameter in the body. Staff accounts can only be created by an authenticated administrator through <code>POST /admin/staff</code>, which is protected by <code>JwtAuthGuard</code> and <code>RolesGuard</code> with <code>@Roles([UserRole.ADMIN])</code>."</i>
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Q: Why do you store money in integer minor units?</b><br>
      <i>A: "Standard floating-point numbers in computers suffer from IEEE 754 precision issues (e.g., 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.30000000000000004). Storing money as integer minor units (such as 250,000 for 2,500.00 BDT) guarantees exact arithmetic with zero rounding error."</i>
    </li>
  </ol>
</div>

